WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.500
Good morning, John!

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.500
Today is the day after Thanksgiving here in America.

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:03.500
Traditionally,

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:04.100
the day

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.000
when we buy

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.500
all the things!

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:07.800
Actually, more traditionally at my house,

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.000
it's the day when we don't leave the house;

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.000
because Black Friday.. terrifies me.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.100
For people who aren't American,

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.200
the very ominous-sounding Black Friday is an accident of history.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.600
So, back in 1621,

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.600
it was hard livin' here in America;

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:19.800
and the first English settlers

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.700
had a really bountiful crop.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:22.300
Apparently.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.800
This is somewhat mythological...

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.000
And, it was on a Thursday;

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.000
and they were thankful!

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:27.000
That inspired a holiday;

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.000
which is a nice kind of idea for a holiday,

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.000
where you just talk about the things that you're thankful for.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:31.800
And eat a bunch of food!

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.000
You also eat a bunch of food.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:33.500
Until the 1860's,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.000
different states celebrated this holiday on different days.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.800
Until Abraham Lincoln said:

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.800
"This.. is the day! We're gonna have a national day.";

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.000
and, the South was like:

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.000
"We're not even part of you anymore.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.200
What are you talking about?!"

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.500
But then, when we got back together again,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:47.000
that was good;

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.000
and it became the national holiday.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.000
The fourth Thursday in November.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Now, because it's a national holiday on the Thursday,

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.000
you also sort of get that Friday as a de facto day off.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.000
'Cause you don't want to go back to work for one day

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:58.500
and then have the weekend;

00:00:58.500 --> 00:00:59.500
that's just dumb!

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.200
In the '60's [1960's], because so many people were going on vacation on this day,

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.500
or, to a lesser extent, going shopping;

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.500
this became such a terrible traffic mess

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.500
that police and taxi drivers started calling it "Black Friday".

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:10.500
As stores,

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.700
and also just general consumerist cultures,

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.500
started pushing the idea of Christmas shopping

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.000
further and further away from Christmas;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:18.500
the big block in the road,

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.500
where you can't push it back any further, is Thanksgiving.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.000
'Cause that's its own holiday.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.000
You can't start Christmas before Thanksgiving.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.200
And so, Black Friday became the official tipping point

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:30.000
into the consumerist bonanza that is the holiday shopping season!

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.800
Despite the fact that it has kind of this ominous-sounding name,

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:38.000
it has been billed as the biggest and most important and best holiday shopping day of the year;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:38.800
which is actually not true.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:40.800
We are.. a nation of procrastinators;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.800
and thus, the biggest shopping day of the year

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.000
is Christmas Eve.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.500
It seems a little bit upsetting

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.000
that the day after we all get done

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.500
telling the world why we should be satisfied with our lives,

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.300
we are.. barraged with an advertising blitz

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.500
showing us all of the things that we lack!

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:56.200
At the same time,

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.200
it is very important to the American economy.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.100
I mean, you can't really get angry at people

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.200
for wanting to give each other things...

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:02.700
And yes,

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:06.000
DFTBA.com has its own Black Friday and Cyber Monday deals;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.500
and you can go to DFTBA.com and check those out now.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:09.500
It's also

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.500
our 4th Anniversary of being a company,

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:12.500
which is very exciting!

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:13.800
And, to celebrate that fourth anniversary,

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.800
all shipping within the United States this weekend,

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.100
starting tomorrow,

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.000
I think,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.000
is just $4.00!

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.000
No matter what you buy!!

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.000
But, I do think it's important that

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.000
we don't just scrub the idea of satisfaction and thankfulness out of our brains

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.500
as soon as we're done for Thanksgiving.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.000
And so, on this Black Friday,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.000
I want to continue being thankful.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.000
I'm thankful to my wonderful and supportive parents;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.800
to my beautiful, and intelligent, and hilarious wife.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.500
I'm thankful to my new Wii U.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.000
If you want to go to youtube.com/hankgames,

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.000
you can see us playing the new Super Mario Brothers there soon.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.500
I'm thankful to all of my great friends here in Missoula

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.000
and across the world.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.800
I'm thankful to finally have a robust and stable forum

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.300
that can handle the weight of Nerdfighteria.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:51.800
There's a link in the description.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.200
I'm thankful to Youtube and Google

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.000
for funding SciShow and Crash Course,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.000
and for supporting

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.000
VlogBrothers, and all of our endeavors through the years.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.000
I'm thankful to Reddit ,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:00.700
and QI,

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:02.000
and Mental Floss magazine,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.200
for making sure that I keep learning interesting things.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.800
I'm thankful to Jonas Salk for giving away the patent to his polio vaccine to the world,

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.200
so that it could be less expensive to manufacture;

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.800
and Tim Berners-Lee for doing basically the same thing with the internet.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.500
I'm thankful to the Nerdcrafteria Minecraft server and Tumblr, and the Ning;

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:19.500
and all the other places

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:22.000
on the internet where Nerdfighters do Nerdfighter things.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.700
And, I'm thankful to all the wonderful, thoughtful, intelligent people

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.700
who watch and support

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.200
the things that I do on the internet;

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:32.500
even when it's sappy, silly videos like this one.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.000
And I'm thankful, of course,

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:34.500
for my brother

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:37.700
who's always driving me to do new and interesting and difficult things;

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.700
and who's raising a wonderful family,

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:43.000
and who says wise and intelligent things on Tumblr.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.000
And John,

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.600
I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.600
*Music plays*

